@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the Chinese medical canon
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and the route that had to be decided before
# its first node, then the needling and the two sets of prohibitions that hang
# on it, then the disagreement the transmitted text carries about its own
# death intervals, then the two signs by which the operation is said to have
# taken and the one condition under which the source says it cannot take at
# all, then the one capacity in this corpus that comes with a test procedure,
# and last the regimen and the graded persons of the opening chapter.
#
# The tradition instance was registered in traditions/registry-east-asian.ttl
# and is moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# Opened from the independence entrance of SPEC §14: it stood at
# pm:contactRouteAbsent, which is the value that makes a witness countable.
# The reading corrected the entrance rather than confirming it; the reason is
# on the instance.
#

pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinese medical canon"@en, "Chinesischer medizinischer Kanon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The canonical Chinese transmission of needling, of the qi and the vessels, and of the spirits held to reside in the depots, set out as dialogues between the Yellow Lord and his interlocutors and carrying, alongside the doctrine, its own prohibitions, its own intervals to death and its own signs by which an operation is said to have taken."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance, and the reading corrected that entrance. The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the route runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The received text is not the Han-period text but the recension Wang Bing completed in seven hundred and sixty-two as the editors of ten sixty-seven passed it on, and that recension is constituted by its commentary: the commentary printed with the opening chapter quotes the Laozi twice and names the Heshanggong commentary on it, and the commentary elsewhere in the same juan quotes the Zhuangzi. The Laozi and the Zhuangzi are the corpus the Daoist file in this graph rests part of its material on. An agreement between this file and that one is therefore not two witnesses at the level of the transmitted text, whatever may hold of the stratum beneath it."@en ;
    skos:note "The correction is narrower than the value can express, and the difference matters for any convergence built on this file. What is documented is a route at the point where the corpus was fixed in the form read, in the eighth century and again in the eleventh. Whether the first-century material that the compilation rests on had such a route is a separate question, and it is not decided here; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers. The corresponding question for the Indian and the Greek material was searched and stands as its own issue."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, in the Chinese: Suwen 1, 14, 16, 26, 52 and 54, and Lingshu 8, 9 and 73, each in the juan named in the citations below. The Song editors' collation notes printed with Suwen 16 and Suwen 52 were read with them and carry the dispute node in this file. The parallel in the Zhenjiu jiayi jing was opened for the same passage and is cited as the second witness there. The remainder of both classics, and the whole of the Nanjing, were searched by term and not read continuously; what that turned up and did not verify stands in the issues named in the harvest note, not here."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition note. Both classics were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, which is the received text and not a critical edition: for the Suwen the Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen with Wang Bing's commentary and the Song collation notes, for the Lingshu the Huangdi suwen lingshu jing. The digitisation used names the collection and not the printing within it, and the citations say so by naming the collection. Where a reading is contested the contest is entered as a node and not smoothed into the citation."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Huangdi neijing (Suwen and Lingshu); Nanjing; Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing; Yang Shangshan, Huangdi neijing taisu" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

#
# The needling and its prohibitions
#

pmp:Cizhen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Needling"@en, "Nadelung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The insertion of a needle at a named place on the body to a stated depth, held to move the qi of a depot or a vessel either towards fullness or towards emptiness. The corpus treats it throughout as an operation that can go wrong, and states the going wrong in the same register as the going right."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:CizhongWuzang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Striking a depot: the interval and the sign"@en, "Der Treffer ins Depot: die Frist und das Zeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A needle driven into one of the depots is said to kill after a stated interval, and each interval is paired with a bodily sign by which the strike is to be recognised. The heart: death in one day, the movement belching. The liver: five days, speech. The kidney: six days, sneezing. The lung: three days, coughing. The spleen: ten days, swallowing. The gall bladder: a day and a half, vomiting. The same chapter continues with strikes that maim rather than kill and names the outcome for each: into the brain through the occipital opening, death on the spot; the vessel in the face, blindness; the marrow between the vertebrae, a bent back; the knee cap, lameness; the arm's great yin vessel, immediate death from loss of blood."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the form and not the content. This is a claim that names an outcome it excludes: a person struck in the heart who is alive on the third day is not covered by any weaker reading of the sentence, and the sign attached to each interval says what the strike is supposed to look like while the interval runs. Very little in this graph is stated that way."@en ;
    skos:note "The intervals are not stable across the witnesses, and the transmitted text says so about itself. That is held in the dispute node below rather than in this definition, which gives the numbers as the recension read prints them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:WucijinQizhuang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The seven states in which one does not needle"@en, "Die sieben Zustände, in denen nicht genadelt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seven conditions of the person are named in which the needle is not to be used, and the first two carry their consequence with them: do not needle one greatly drunk, it makes the qi disordered; do not needle one greatly angered, it makes the qi go against its course; do not needle one greatly exhausted, nor one newly filled with food, nor one greatly hungry, nor one greatly thirsty, nor one greatly startled."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated for the first two and withheld for the remaining five, which the file keeps as it stands rather than extending the pattern. Where a later chapter of the same corpus states consequences for the whole list, that is a second and differently worded passage and it is entered separately."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:ShierjinZhongshi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve prohibitions, and what is said to follow from breaking them"@en, "Die zwölf Verbote und was ihr Bruch bewirken soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "The needling chapter of the Lingshu states the same prohibitions as a two-sided rule and adds a state the other list does not have. Newly after intercourse do not needle, and newly needled do not lie together; already drunk do not needle, and already needled do not get drunk; newly angered, newly exhausted, already filled, already hungry, already thirsty, each in the same double form. On great fright and great fear the rule is not a prohibition at all but a condition: settle the qi first, then needle. Two further clauses set waiting times by the manner of arrival, the time of a meal for one who came by cart and the time of walking ten li for one who came on foot. The chapter then names what breaking the twelve does: the vessels are disordered and the qi scattered, a yang illness enters the yin and a yin illness comes out as yang, the evil qi is generated afresh, and the text calls it hewing at the body, wasting the brain marrow and losing the qi."@en ;
    skos:note "The relation to the Suwen list is not similarity but disagreement, and the Song collation note printed with the Suwen chapter says so in as many words: it states that the seven clauses there and the Lingshu clauses go in and out of one another, and it prints the Lingshu wording beside them so that a reader can see it. The two differ on fright, which one forbids and the other admits after a delay, and on intercourse, which only the Lingshu has."@en ;
    skos:note "This is also the passage in this corpus that comes closest to a stated failure condition for the operator rather than the patient. It says what the transgression produces, in the body, and names the direction of the change."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

#
# The corpus disagreeing with itself about its own intervals
#

pmc:CizhongIntervalDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "How long after a depot is struck death is said to follow"@en, "Wie lange nach dem Treffer ins Depot der Tod eintreten soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three places in the same canon give different intervals for the same strike, and a fourth witness gives a fourth set. The needling-prohibition chapter has the kidney at six days, the lung at three and the spleen at ten. The chapter on the essentials of examination has the heart dying within one circuit rather than in one day, the spleen at five days, the kidney at seven, the lung at five, and adds a strike through the diaphragm after which the patient dies within the year however well he seems to recover. The Zhenjiu jiayi jing has the kidney at three days, the spleen at fifteen, and gives the liver's sign as a different character from the one the received Suwen prints. The Song editors record in their collation notes that the older recension of Quan Yuanqi agreed with the Jiayi jing on both numbers, and that Wang Bing changed the liver's sign."@en ;
    skos:note "The dispute is entered because the transmitted text states it about itself and states a verdict on it. The collation note printed with the chapter on the essentials of examination says that these three discourses are all the words of Qi Bo and that their differing is an error of transmission. That is one party's account of the divergence and not a neutral finding, and the graph keeps it as such."@en ;
    skos:note "The commentators do not treat the numbers as observations. The note beside the spleen argues from ten being the completion number of earth, and other commentators have wanted to correct liver and lung to the generation numbers of wood and metal. A number defended from a numerology is not thereby false, but it is not evidence of a counted interval either, and a protocol on this claim would have to begin there."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for this graph is narrower than the dispute. Nothing here says that the intervals are wrong; what it says is that a citation naming one chapter conceals that the same canon carries three answers, and that the earliest reachable witness disagrees with the received text at two of the six places."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:CizhongWuzang ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14", "Huangdi neijing suwen 16 (Zhen yao jing zhong lun), with the Xin jiao zheng collation notes, in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4", "Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing, juan 5 (Zhen jiu jin ji), in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

#
# What is said to show that it took, and what is said to make it impossible
#

pmc:QizhiYouxiao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The arrival of the qi, and the sign that is refused"@en, "Das Ankommen des Qi und das zurückgewiesene Zeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Lingshu states what it means for the qi to have arrived and to have had effect, and states it as a rule over the vessel and not over the patient. Where the operation drained, the vessel is to be as large as before but no longer hard; if it is hard as before, then although the patient says it is as it was, the illness has not gone. Where the operation supplemented, the vessel is to be as large as before and harder; if it is as before and not hard, then although the patient says he feels relieved, the illness has not gone. The chapter closes the rule from the other side: even where the pain does not follow the needle, the illness must be in retreat."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for entering this rather than the many other success signs in the corpus. A success sign told to the practised beforehand is worth little, because he will report it; this passage names such a report and refuses it twice over, in the same sentence in which it gives the criterion that replaces it. The corpus therefore holds a stated distinction between what a patient says and what counts as the operation having taken, and the distinction runs against the interest of the operator."@en ;
    skos:note "What the passage does not supply is a way of reading the vessel that two examiners would read alike. The criterion is hardness under the finger, which is why this is entered as a sign the tradition names and not as a procedure the graph can use."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

pmc:ZhenxiaReHan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heat and cold under the needle"@en, "Wärme und Kälte unter der Nadel"@de ;
    skos:definition "A second sign, stated in the chapter that explains the nine needles: where emptiness is to be filled, it is warm under the needle, for the qi becomes full and then it is warm; where fullness is to be drained, it is cold under the needle, for the qi becomes empty and then it is cold. The same chapter makes the warmth the criterion for withdrawal, saying that in needling an emptiness one waits for the fullness, and that when the yang qi has arrived in force and it is warm under the needle, then the needle is taken out."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the vessel criterion above, and the difference is the point. This one is felt by the operator at the needle and is not stated to be checkable by anybody else; the other is a rule over a finding that at least two people can take. A tradition that carries both is a tradition that has not settled which of them decides, and the file does not settle it either."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 54 (Zhen jie pian), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:Jingshen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The essence-spirit and the intent of the sick person"@en, "Essenzgeist und Absicht des Kranken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition of the patient on which the corpus makes the whole operation depend: the essence and the spirit, and with them the intent, held to be present or absent and to be won or lost. The Lingshu instructs the needler to observe the bearing of the sick person in order to know whether essence, spirit, hun and po are present or gone; where these five are damaged, it says, the needle cannot treat him."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

pmc:Shenbushi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The spirit does not lend itself"@en, "Der Geist gibt sich nicht her"@de ;
    skos:definition "The term the corpus gives to the case in which the body is worn out and the blood exhausted and the treatment establishes nothing. Asked why that happens, the answer is that the spirit does not lend itself. Needle and stone, the passage continues, are a way; where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured; and where the essence is ruined and the spirit gone, the constructive and defensive qi can no longer be gathered back, because desire is without end and grief without cease."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the failure condition of this corpus that is hardest to test and most worth recording, because it is located in the patient and not in the operation. Any outcome of a treatment can be assigned to it after the fact, and the passage supplies no mark by which its presence could be established beforehand. Recorded as the tradition's own account of why a correct procedure fails, and marked here as the shape of claim that absorbs every outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" .

pmc:CizhenPresupposesJingshen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Needling presupposes that the spirit is present"@en, "Die Nadelung setzt voraus, dass der Geist da ist"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Jingshen ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Two passages state it and both state a consequence rather than a degree. Where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured, and the body worn out and the blood exhausted the treatment establishes nothing. Where the five, essence, spirit, hun, po and the intent, are damaged, the needle cannot treat him at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4", "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

#
# The one capacity in this corpus that comes with a test
#

pmc:Shoudu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The poisonous hand"@en, "Die giftige Hand"@de ;
    skos:definition "A property of a person's hands, named in the chapter that assigns offices according to fitness: some have bitter nails and poisonous hands and in doing things are apt to injure, and such a person is to be set to pressing on accumulations and holding down numbness. The chapter opposes it to the sweet hand. It stands in a list beside the assignment of the well-jointed and supple-sinewed to the leading and pulling of the qi, and of the sharp-tongued and slighting to the spitting on abscesses and the incanting of illnesses."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is that the difference is a property of the person and not of what he does, since the chapter assigns offices by it and says that where the person is not the right one the work does not succeed and his teacher goes without a name."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11" .

pmp:AnguiShi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The pressing of the turtle"@en, "Die Probe an der Schildkröte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the chapter gives for deciding whether a person has the poisonous hand. A turtle is placed under a vessel and the person presses on the vessel from above. If his hand is poisonous, the turtle is dead in fifty days; if his hand is sweet, it lives on as before."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice in its own right because of what it is: a discrimination procedure, stated by the tradition, over a claim about a person, with a fixed term and two outcomes that are not the same. Nothing else read in this corpus in this run has that shape, and the reason for opening the corpus from the testability side was the expectation of finding it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11" .

#
# The regimen and the graded persons of the opening chapter
#

pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The regimen of those who knew the way"@en, "Das Regimen derer, die den Weg kannten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The conduct the opening chapter ascribes to the people of high antiquity who knew the way: they took the yin and the yang as their model and were in accord with the arts and the numbers, they were measured in food and drink and regular in rising and resting, and they did not work recklessly. The chapter states the counter-case in the same breath, of the people of the present who take wine for water, take the reckless for the regular, enter the bedchamber drunk, exhaust their essence in desire and are without measure in rising and resting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The hundred years fully lived out"@en, "Die vollständig gelebten hundert Jahre"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the same chapter attaches to that conduct: form and spirit together, the heaven-given years carried to their end, a hundred years passed through and then departure. Against it stands the stated outcome of the counter-case, decline at half a hundred."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a capacity claim and not as a piece of cosmology because the chapter states both arms and states them in the same terms. It is nonetheless not a claim any protocol reaches from here: the antecedent is a manner of living over a lifetime and the corpus supplies no way of establishing that it was kept."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:YangshengYieldsDubaisui a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The regimen yields the full span"@en, "Das Regimen bringt die volle Spanne hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:ZhenrenZhirenShengrenXianren a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The four graded persons of the first chapter"@en, "Die vier gestuften Personen des ersten Kapitels"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four grades, each with a stated reach and each ascribed to a conduct rather than to a birth. The true persons of high antiquity lifted and held heaven and earth, grasped the yin and the yang, breathed the essence-qi, stood alone and guarded the spirit, and their flesh was as one, so that their years wore out heaven and earth and had no ending time. Next the perfected persons, whose virtue was whole, who were in accord with yin and yang and in tune with the four seasons, who left the age and departed from custom, who accumulated the essence and made the spirit whole, who roamed between heaven and earth and whose seeing and hearing went beyond the eight reaches. Next the sages, who dwelt in the harmony of heaven and earth and followed the pattern of the eight winds, whose bodily form was not worn out and whose essence and spirit were not scattered, and who likewise could be counted in hundreds. Last the worthies, who took heaven and earth for their rule and modelled themselves on sun and moon, and who could lengthen their years but had a limit."@en ;
    skos:note "The two upper grades carry the claims that make this a capacity list rather than a moral one: a lifespan coextensive with heaven and earth and without end, and a seeing and hearing that reaches beyond the eight quarters. The two lower grades carry no such claim and are bounded in the text itself, the third at a count in hundreds and the fourth explicitly at a limit."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the chapter says that the two upper grades were observed, and nothing says how one would tell a perfected person from a worthy. The grades are entered as what the corpus claims and not as a scale the graph can place anyone on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:Dujian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing alone where all are looking"@en, "Allein sehen, wo alle hinsehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The corpus is asked what it means by spirit and answers with a description of a perception that precedes the senses. The ear does not hear; the eye is bright and the heart opens and the intent goes first; he understands alone and clearly and the mouth cannot say it; all look and he alone sees; it is as though dark, and he alone is bright, as when wind blows the clouds away. The chapter sets this against the corresponding account of form, where what is sought is found by asking after the illness and searching it in the vessels."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the clearest statement in this corpus that a capacity of the practitioner is meant and not a technique. It is also the least examinable: the passage says in the same breath that the mouth cannot say it, so nothing in the description could be set against an outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" .

pmc:SituatingDujianAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Das Alleinsehen wird bei abgezogener Vertiefung verortet"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Dujian ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The passage names the withdrawal of the ordinary channel itself and does not leave it to be inferred: the ear does not hear, and where all look he alone sees. The practitioner is awake and at work on a patient throughout, so what is set aside is the reliance on what the senses report and not waking itself. That is the criterion this coordinate names."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" .
